# The retired bare-metal deployment's Rails log, under the same promise.
#
# /srv/www/org-openipc served openipc.org until the containerization on
# 2026-08-23, and nothing has written to this directory since 2026-08-24. What
# is in it is Rails production logs, and Rails writes the client address on
# every request -- `Started GET "/" for 1.2.3.4`. Measured on 2026-09-21: 152
# files, 90 MB, dating back to 2022-07-14, with 186,313 address-carrying lines
# in the largest single file.
#
# So the claim in #227 that the nginx access log is "the one place on this host
# that holds visitor addresses" was wrong by seven times in bytes, and this is
# the larger half of what the privacy page is promising about. One promise,
# both directories, the same fourteen days.
#
# The host file this replaces asked for `rotate 365` and reloaded apache2
# afterwards -- a policy from 2022 that nobody has chosen since, and a daemon
# this host does not run. The `notifempty` it also carried is why the directory
# is frozen: these files stopped rotating on the day they stopped being
# written, which is how a directory nobody looks at keeps four years of
# addresses.
#
# `dateext` is kept because the archives already on disk are named that way
# (production.log-20260331.gz); maxage prunes them under that scheme, which
# was checked against a copy of the real naming before this was written.
#
# Installing this deletes everything in that directory older than fourteen
# days on the next logrotate run. That is the point of it, and it is why
# deploy/install-logrotate.sh counts and reports those files rather than
# quietly removing them.
/srv/www/org-openipc/log/*.log {
	daily
	missingok
	rotate 14
	maxage 14
	dateext
	compress
	delaycompress
	create 0640 root adm
	sharedscripts
}
